From: Jaime Dias <jaimejosediasnt@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 16:40:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 49/49] vcgfx: clear the page it is about to scan out

Whatever was on this display belonged to whoever had it before, and was
drawn for a different mode. The firmware can hand back the same memory at
a new depth -- 16bpp to 32bpp is what happens here -- and then those
bytes are not old content, they are noise: the same pixels reappear as
two half-width copies of themselves. That is the doubled splash seen on
a Pi 3 and under QEMU, and it is not a rendering bug in whoever drew
them; they were drawn correctly for the mode that was live at the time.

A driver that programs a mode and leaves the previous owner's bytes on
screen is displaying something it did not draw. Clearing them is the only
honest thing it can do, and it costs one pass over the framebuffer at
mode-set time. The back page has been cleared for this exact reason all
along -- "so the first flip can't expose stale memory" -- and the front
page needed the same sentence.

This does not restore a splash across the mode change; nothing here can.
The boot presentation drew into a surface this driver has now taken, and
it is not the driver's business to keep somebody else's picture alive.
What it removes is the machine showing a mangled version of that picture
and calling it a display.
--- a/arch/arm-native/soc/broadcom/2708/hidd/vcgfx/vcgfx_onbitmap.c
+++ b/arch/arm-native/soc/broadcom/2708/hidd/vcgfx/vcgfx_onbitmap.c
@@ -282,6 +282,24 @@
     xsd->vcsd_FBFront      = 0;
     xsd->vcsd_FBPages      = 1;
 
+    /*
+     * Clear the page that is about to be scanned out.
+     *
+     * Whatever was on this display belonged to whoever had it before, and it
+     * was drawn for a different mode. The firmware can hand back the same
+     * memory at a new depth -- 16bpp to 32bpp here -- and then those bytes are
+     * not old content, they are noise: the same pixels reappear as two
+     * half-width copies of themselves, which is exactly what a Pi 3 and QEMU
+     * both showed after this driver took over.
+     *
+     * A driver that programs a mode and leaves the previous owner's bytes on
+     * screen is displaying something it did not draw. Clearing is the only
+     * honest thing it can do with them, and it costs one pass over the
+     * framebuffer at mode-set time. The back page below is cleared for the
+     * same reason, and has been all along.
+     */
+    memset((APTR)(IPTR)xsd->vcsd_FBPage[0], 0, fb_pitch * height);
+
     if (pages == 2)
     {
         /* Only trust flipping if the firmware honours SETVOFFSET
